bynh-status-agent under systemd
========================

Requires systemd 247 or newer (LoadCredential with DynamicUser): Debian 11+,
Ubuntu 22.04+, RHEL/Rocky/Alma 9+, Fedora, Arch.

The quickest path is install.sh at the repository root, which does all of
the steps below. To do it by hand:

1. Install the binary

     install -m 0755 bynh-status-agent-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl /usr/local/bin/bynh-status-agent

2. Store the token in a root-only file (it is handed to the service as a
   systemd credential, so the agent's dynamic user never needs to read /etc):

     install -d -m 0755 /etc/bynh-status-agent
     ( umask 077; read -rs -p 'bynh agent token: ' t; printf '%s\n' "$t" > /etc/bynh-status-agent/token )

3. Optional settings in /etc/bynh-status-agent/bynh-status-agent.toml (no secrets in it):

     api_url = "https://api.bynh.io"
     allow_private = false   # true for on-premise agents checking internal hosts
     report_ip = true
     concurrency = 64

   The file is optional; every key can also be set with BYNH_* variables
   in a drop-in (systemctl edit bynh-status-agent):

     [Service]
     Environment=BYNH_ALLOW_PRIVATE=true

4. Install and start the unit

     install -m 0644 bynh-status-agent.service /etc/systemd/system/bynh-status-agent.service
     systemctl daemon-reload
     systemctl enable --now bynh-status-agent

5. Check it

     systemctl status bynh-status-agent
     journalctl -u bynh-status-agent -f

The unit runs the agent as a dynamic, unprivileged user with no capabilities,
a read-only file system, only IPv4/IPv6/Unix sockets and a 128 MB memory cap.
Rotating the token: write the new one to /etc/bynh-status-agent/token and run
systemctl restart bynh-status-agent.
